🔑 MAIN CONCEPTS YOU LEARN FROM THIS CODE
1️⃣ Prototype Extension (Monkey Patching) ⭐⭐⭐
Array.prototype.groupBy = function(fn) { ... }


📌 Concept:
You are adding a custom method to all arrays in JavaScript.

That means:

[1,2,3].groupBy(...)


works just like:

[1,2,3].map(...)


🧠 What you learn:

Arrays are objects

They inherit methods from Array.prototype

You can extend built-in objects

⚠️ Interview note:

This is powerful but risky in production because it affects ALL arrays.

2️⃣ Higher-Order Functions ⭐⭐⭐
@param {Function} fn


📌 Concept:
A function that takes another function as input.

Here:

const key = fn(item);


🧠 What you learn:

Functions are first-class citizens in JS

You can pass logic as a parameter

This is the foundation of map, filter, reduce

Example:

groupBy(String)
groupBy(x => x % 2)

3️⃣ Dynamic Object Keys ⭐⭐⭐
result[key] = [];


📌 Concept:
You are creating object properties dynamically using a variable.

Equivalent to:

result["1"] = []


🧠 What you learn:

Objects can be used as hash maps

Keys are converted to strings automatically

Similar to HashMap in Java

4️⃣ Accumulator Pattern (Grouping Pattern) ⭐⭐⭐⭐
const result = {};


📌 Concept:
You are building a result step-by-step.

Pattern:

create empty object
→ loop
→ compute key
→ push values
→ return result


🧠 This pattern appears everywhere:

Group By

Frequency maps

Counting problems

Data aggregation

🔥 This is a core DSA + JS pattern

5️⃣ Iteration with for...of ⭐⭐
for (const item of this)


📌 Concept:
Iterates over array values, not indexes.

🧠 Difference:

for...of  → values
for...in  → indexes (not recommended for arrays)

6️⃣ Truthiness / Falsy Check ⭐⭐
if (!result[key]) {
    result[key] = [];
}


📌 Concept:
JavaScript automatically treats:

undefined → false

null → false

So this means:

“If this key doesn’t exist yet, create it”

